<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro I of Brazil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">abdicated <a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Pedro I, Emperor Pedro I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-braganza">House of Braganza</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro I was the founder and first emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, a prince of the Portuguese royal house who proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grito-do-ipiranga">Brazilian independence</a> on 7 September 1822. His reign was turbulent, marked by regional revolts and tension with the Brazilian-born elite. He abdicated in 1831 in favour of his young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a> and returned to Portugal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil.jpg" alt="Pedro I of Brazil" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simplício Rodrigues de Sá · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DpedroI-brasil-full_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…history's more unusual acts of dynastic improvisation, when the Portuguese heir and Prince Regent Pedro, on the banks of the Ipiranga River, a stream near São Paulo, on 7 September 1822, proclaimed Brazilian independence with the Grito do Ipiranga and was acclaimed <b>Emperor Pedro I</b> on 12 October.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Called back to Lisbon by the Liberal Revolution of 1820, John VI left his heir, who refused the Cortes' demand to reduce Brazil to a colony on the Dia do Fico, 9 January 1822, and was crowned <b>Emperor Pedro I</b> after independence on 7 September 1822.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Brazilian independence in 1822 under <b>Pedro I</b>, the son of King João VI, who refused the Lisbon Cortes’ attempts to restore Brazil’s colonial subordination, removed the empire’s economic heartland and left Portugal with African territories that it sought to occupy effectively only during the Scramble for Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Days earlier, on 7 April, Brazil's first emperor, <b>Pedro I</b>, had abdicated, leaving his five-year-old son as Emperor Pedro II, and on 13 April the ship carrying him left the port of Rio de Janeiro.</blockquote><a href="/songs/brazilian-national-anthem">Brazilian National Anthem</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1823 <b>Pedro I</b> dissolved Brazil's Constituent Assembly, and in 1824 he imposed a constitution that gave the emperor a moderating power.</blockquote><a href="/state/confederation-of-the-equator">Confederation of the Equator</a></li></ul></section>
